}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/81/818ad935fd7aa592f00c23c9c77799d127449b4bb6ffacee4354e7acf39887d8.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/70/70e268a08195a92573f41927ae7de653837fca273f0c75cb951243a31ec660a2.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id 17592186535078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c513f0-8e53-5681-addf-29a9147b60ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186534371} {:answer/id #uuid "727c6d10-f05b-51c9-89d4-2264deae7ee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186534506} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0dedae-2946-5360-b334-0d9616cd33c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186534536} {:answer/id 